1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,208
My father died on this icy mountain twenty years ago, but his heart is still beating inside my station radio.

2
00:00:08,508 --> 00:00:17,388
Every midnight, the static clears into a steady rhythmic thump that matches the exact pace of a terrified human pulse.

3
00:00:17,688 --> 00:00:24,960
Company rules strictly forbid leaving the tower after dark, but I grab my climbing gear and trace the signal down.

4
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:33,156
I rappel into a hidden crevasse beneath the station, my flashlight beam slicing through relentless blinding sheets of frozen fog.

5
00:00:33,456 --> 00:00:43,032
At the bottom sits a massive reinforced steel bunker, its heavy hatch half-buried under decades of undisturbed blue glacial ice.

6
00:00:43,332 --> 00:00:52,716
I pry the frozen door open, breathing in stale, metallic air as the glowing radio signal pulses louder right ahead.

7
00:00:53,016 --> 00:01:01,152
Inside, rows of glass cryo-pods line the frost walls, each containing a frozen body wearing my exact brown shearling coat.

8
00:01:01,452 --> 00:01:09,684
I wipe the ice off the nearest pod visor and stare directly at my own face, complete with my right eyebrow scar.

9
00:01:09,984 --> 00:01:17,592
The radio signal isn't coming from my missing father; it's the failing heart monitor of the clone who served right before me.

10
00:01:17,892 --> 00:01:27,372
I was never guarding this mountain peak; I am just another biological battery waiting for my own heart to vanish into static.
